<%# Shown between a guest's message and the reply to it, because several
    seconds of nothing reads as "this is broken" on a phone — the guest has
    no other signal that anything is happening.

    Inside #chat-messages rather than pinned above the composer, so it sits
    where the reply itself will appear and scrolls with the transcript. A
    guest who has scrolled up to reread something does not get a fixed banner
    following them down the screen.

    aria-live="polite" rather than "assertive": a screen-reader user should
    hear this when they reach a natural pause, not have it interrupt the
    message they are still reading. role="status" carries the same meaning
    for anything that does not read aria-live.

    Hidden by default and shown by chat_composer_controller.js — it is not
    rendered per-reply by the server, because the server does not know when
    the guest pressed send, only when the job finished. %>
<div id="typing-indicator" class="hidden items-center gap-2 self-start rounded-lg bg-gray-50 px-3 py-2"
     role="status" aria-live="polite" data-chat-composer-target="typingIndicator">
  <span class="sr-only"><%= t("guest.chats.typing.sr_label") %></span>

  <%# Three dots rather than words, so nothing has to be translated into a
      claim about who is typing. The concierge is not a person and the guest
      surface never says "AI", so any sentence here would have to pick one —
      the animation says "working on it" without asserting either.

      motion-reduce:animate-none, not a media query around the whole element:
      someone who has asked for less motion still gets the indicator, just
      without the bounce. Removing it entirely would take away the
      reassurance the element exists for. %>
  <span class="flex items-center gap-1" aria-hidden="true">
    <% 3.times do |dot| %>
      <span class="size-1.5 animate-bounce rounded-full bg-gray-400 motion-reduce:animate-none"
            style="animation-delay: <%= dot * 150 %>ms"></span>
    <% end %>
  </span>
</div>
